# The  Rep artificial repeat protein scaffold: a new tool for crystallization and live cell applications

## abstract
We have designed a new family of artificial proteins, named aRep, based on HEAT (acronym for Huntingtin, elongation factor 3 (EF3), protein pphosphatase 2A (PP2A), yeast kinase Tor1) repeat proteins containing an a-helical repeated motif. The sequence of the repeated motifs, first identified in a thermostable archae protein was optimized using a consensus design strategy and used for the construction of a library of artificial proteins. All proteins from this library share the same general fold but differ both in the number of repeats and in five highly randomized amino acid positions within each repeat. The randomized side chains altogether provide a hypervariable surface on aRep variants. Sequences from this library are efficiently expressed as soluble, folded and very stable proteins. aRep binders with high affinity for various protein targets were selected by phage display. Low micromolar to nanomolar dissociation constants between partners were measured and the structures of several complexes (specific aRep/protein target) were solved by X-ray crystallography. Using GFP as a model target, it was demonstrated that aReps can be used as bait in pulldown experiments. aReps can be expressed in eukaryotic cells and specifically interact with their target addressed to different cell compartments.

The αRep artificial repeat protein scaffold: a new tool for crystallization and live cell applications
Abstract
We have designed a new family of artificial proteins, named αRep, based on HEAT (acronym for Huntingtin, elongation factor 3 (EF3), protein pphosphatase 2A (PP2A), yeast kinase Tor1) repeat proteins containing an $\alpha$ -helical repeated motif. The sequence of the repeated motifs, first identified in a thermostable archae protein was optimized using a consensus design strategy and used for the construction of a library of artificial proteins. All proteins from this library share the same general fold but differ both in the number of repeats and in five highly randomized amino acid positions within each repeat. The randomized side chains altogether provide a hypervariable surface on αRep variants. Sequences from this library are efficiently expressed as soluble, folded and very stable proteins. αRep binders with high affinity for various protein targets were selected by phage display. Low micromolar to nanomolar dissociation constants between partners were measured and the structures of several complexes (specific αRep/protein target) were solved by X-ray crystallography. Using GFP as a model target, it was demonstrated that $\alpha$ Reps can be used as bait in pulldown experiments. αReps can be expressed in eukaryotic cells and specifically interact with their target addressed to different cell compartments.
## Introduction
## Directed evolution of protein scaffolds
A long-term goal of protein engineering research is to create new artificial proteins able to interact specifically with predefined biological molecular ‘targets’. The most advanced way to create such specific proteins is currently to construct a very diverse library by random substitution of surface positions of a starting ‘scaffold’ protein [1]. The variants that interact with any chosen target can then be selected using selection methods such as phage, yeast or ribosome display. This general strategy has been successfully used with different scaffold proteins and could therefore be generally applicable. However, the design and experimental construction of an efficient protein library remains a difficult task. The main problem is that a sufficient sequence surface diversification of the starting scaffold is necessary to allow emergence of new binding sites. But practical experiments show that mutations are frequently destabilizing and the extent of random sequence changes is correlated with destabilization of the modified protein [2]. Therefore, the creation of an efficient protein library has to incorporate a fundamental tradeoff between creating enough diversity whereas maintaining stability in a significant fraction of library members. A very elegant and efficient way to bypass this problem was inspired by the evolutionary success of natural proteins families made from repeated self-compatible modules.
## Natural and designed repeat proteins
Several natural protein families [TPR (tetratricopeptide repeat), LRR (leucine rich repeat), ankyrin, HEAT (acronym for Huntingtin, elongation factor 3 (EF3), protein pphosphatase 2A (PP2A), yeast kinase Tor1), armadillo) are constituted by a repeated sequence motif (from 20 to 40 amino acids) [3]. The concatenated motifs, characteristic of each protein family, fold in similar and compatible modules that stack on each other to form solenoid-like structures. Within a protein, all repeats have closely related sequences except at some specific surface positions where a range of amino acids is allowed without compromising the stability of the motif. These variable positions located at equivalent positions of consecutive motifs are therefore structurally juxtaposed in the folded protein. Recombination of existing motifs creates new extended hypervariable surface without compromising protein stability. This principle can efficiently simplify the emergence of new protein–protein interactions. As an example, proteins of the LRR family form the basis of the adaptive immune system present in jawless vertebrates.
The construction of efficient synthetic protein libraries based on repeat proteins was first demonstrated by the Pl ¨uckthun’s group with designed ankyrin repeat proteins (DARPins). Other repeat families, based on TPR, armadillo,
LRR, have since been explored as a source of new artificial specific proteins [4]. This review is focused on the design, characterizations and applications of a family of artificial proteins named αRep whose structure is based on HEATlike helicoidal repeats.
## Design of αRep: a family of artificial HEAT-like repeat proteins
HEAT repeat proteins are ubiquitous and involved in wide range of protein–protein interactions. The most studied subgroups of HEAT repeat proteins are found in eukaryotes and contain long repeated (typically 39 amino acids) and often irregular repeats. αRep proteins are not based on standard eukaryotic HEAT repeat sequence but on a less studied group of HEAT-like repeats commonly found in prokaryotes, including thermophile species, constituted by shorter and regular motifs [5]. The first known structure of a protein from this group (Mth187 PDB: 1TE4) originating from a thermophilic archaea, was used as a starting point to identify a stable form of this repeated motif. Sequence homologues search for proteins made by a concatenation of this type of repeats led to a diverse but coherent collection of repeats. These sequences form a sharply contrasted sequence profile in which most sequence positions are occupied by highly conserved amino acids whereas some others positions appear highly variable (Figure 1). The most variable positions of the repeats are found on the outside surface of helix 2, located on the concave face of the natural protein. These clustered hypervariable positions suggested that randomizing these positions on consecutive motifs could generate a promising versatile binding surface.
Elongated repeat proteins need special motifs, called Nand C-caps, in which the non-polar surfaces, responsible for internal repeat–repeat interactions, are exchanged for exposed hydrophilic surfaces in order to mask the hydrophobic core at both extremities of the solenoids. The C-cap motif used for αRep libraries is the natural C-cap of the protein used as a starting model. However, this model protein does not have a stable N-cap. A putative N-cap sequence was therefore identified from sequence database and later proved an efficient N-Cap motif.
## αRep library construction and improvements
The first experimental library based on this sequence design was assembled by concatenation of a pool of synthetic microgenes, each micro-gene coding for one repeat. The sequence was designed with six partially randomized positions corresponding to the hypervariable positions in natural repeats. The distribution of amino acids at these positions, in natural repeats, is variable but not random and the spectrum of allowed amino acids is clearly position-dependent. For example, proline is the most common amino acid in position 18, at the N-end of the second helix, whereas it is completely absent at position 26 located in the middle of this helix. This naturally biased distribution results from selection pressures that favour structurally stabilizing side chains at each position as well as amino acids prone to be involved in protein– protein interactions. Amino acids frequencies in the library were therefore adjusted at each variable position in order to mimic the natural distribution. This was achieved using appropriately balanced mixture of oligonucleotides, with partially degenerated codons in the variable positions.
The pool of micro-genes was directionally polymerized, in between the ${\bf N}-$ and the C-caps motifs, using non-symmetric cohesive ends. In the resulting library the number of motifs differs between constructs, from 1 to approximately 15 internal motifs inserted between ${\bf N}-$ and C-caps, with a majority of constructs with 2–3 inserted modules.
The first assembled αRep library was useful to test the properties of the proteins. Proteins randomly selected from this library were expressed and shown to be soluble, cooperatively folded and extremely stable (with $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ melting temperatures from $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ up to more than $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The stability varies for each protein but clearly increases with the number of motifs. The crystal structures of some αRep proteins were solved and indicated that the proteins are folded exactly as expected. In Figure 1, the structure of an αRep (name A3) randomly picked from the library is presented. αReps are folded as a succession of $\alpha$ -helices pairs stacking on each other. The succession of repeats forms a flattened right-handed superhelix presenting a curvature along the long axis of the super helix, as observed for other $\alpha$ -solenoids. The internal repeats have nearly identical structures, with the exception of the variable side chains. The convex face of the protein is made by helix 1 of successive repeats and the concave face of the protein is made by the succession of the external surfaces of helices 2.
The N-cap includes randomized amino acids in positions structurally equivalent to the randomized positions of the internal repeats and is folded as a pair of $\alpha$ -helices similarly to internal motifs. The C-cap packing differs from the packing between internal repeats, with its helix 2 (not randomized in the library) located not in the continuity but behind the potential binding surface of internal repeats.
This library was further improved [6], as sequence analysis of randomly selected clones showed that a significant fraction $(30\%)$ of the modules contained sequence errors due to oligonucleotide synthesis or assembly, leading to frame shifts. As the error probability increased with the number of repeats, this library encoded only a low proportion of long proteins with fully correct sequences. In order to increase the fraction of long error-free proteins encoded in the library, a procedure based on ORF selection and subsequent recombination of the subset of in-frame repeats proved very efficient: the initial library was constructed in a phage display vector, in such a way that the proteins are anchored on the phage surface by the C-terminal extremity and are fused to an accessible Flag-tag located at the other end (N-terminal) of the protein. Coding sequences, without any frame shift, were therefore selected using affinity selection on an immobilized anti-Flag antibody. The modules extracted from this subset of clones were almost error-free and could be efficiently amplified and recombined to recreate a highly diverse library that comprises error-free coding sequences, including for long proteins.
αRep repeats were defined using as a structure template (Mth187) a stable, disulfide free, protein from a thermophilic archaea. This protein was the first known structure of this stable form of HEAT-like repeats common in prokaryotic organisms. The sequences of repeats from this protein were used to identify a set of homologous proteins made by this type of self-compatible repeats and then to define a consensus sequence associated to this structure. Characteristic sequences features of this repeat family are summarized in the sequence logo. Most positions are highly conserved whereas a set of amino acids (stars) located on the outside surface of the second helix are hypervariable. αRep library was assembled by polymerization of micro-genes coding for the repeated modules between external modules (N- and C-cap). The micro genes include variable codons for those positions that are hypervariable in this repeat group. The resulting $\alpha\mathrm{{Rep}}$ library has $1.7\times10^{9}$ independent clones which differ by the numbers of inserted motifs and by the sequence of randomized positions of each motif. Proteins from the library are very well expressed, soluble, thermostable and folded as expected. The structure of one of this protein is shown. This protein has four internal motifs and two capping motifs (N-cap shown in green, C-cap in red). The randomized surface of the protein (shown in orange) covers the concave face of the protein.
For each complex, αRep is shown in red (N-cap and C-cap) and orange (internal repeats) and the target is shown in purple (A3, NCS-3.24, FNE) or in green (EGFP) (A) Crystal structure of the complex between two αReps: A3–bA3-2 (one internal repeat (pdb ID 4JW2). (B) Structure of a mutated form of NCS-3.24 in complex with an αRep bNCS324-16 (pdb ID 4JW3). (C) Structure of two complexes between EGFP and bGFP-A (six internal repeats; pdb ID 4XL5) or bGFP-C (three internal repeats; pdb ID 4XVP). These two αReps interact with their EGFP target in completely different orientations. The helices of bGFP-A are oriented perpendicularly to the barrel axis of EGFP whereas the helices of bGFP-C are parallel with this axis. (D) Structure of the FNE–bFNE (three internal repeats) complex (1.8 Å resolution; pdb ID 4PFG). This protein was crystallized only when complexed to a specific αRep.
## Selection of specific protein binders
This optimized library was used to select by phage display αReps binding specifically to a wide range of pre-defined soluble or membrane protein targets. Our phage display procedures generally include three rounds of panning followed by a screening step by phage-ELISA on 96 individual clones. With robust soluble proteins targets [e.g., αRep-A3, NCS-3.24 (neocarzinostatin), EBS1 (saccharomyces cerevisiae Ebs1 protein), FNE (fibronectinbinding protein) . . . ], their direct immobilization on plastic wells was sufficient to recover specific αRep binders [6,7]. In some cases however, positive clones obtained by phageELISA failed to reveal αReps binding to the soluble targets. Assuming that the targets were incorrectly folded upon direct adsorption on ELISA plate, protein targets were in this cases biotinylated and immobilized on streptavidincoated wells. Protein targets expressed in soluble form in Escherichia coli were biotinylated in vivo using a specific sequence (Avi-tag) added at the N-terminal end of the protein. The addition of the tobacco etch virus (TEV) cleavage site between the Avi-Tag and the protein sequence can help for a specific elution of the bound phages by TEV protease. This approach was used to select GFP binders with nanomolar $K_{\mathrm{D}}$ values [6]. For membrane protein targets, biotinylated amphipols were explored as universal ‘velcro’’ to stabilize and immobilize the proteins before incubation with the αRepdisplaying phages. This alternative panning approach applied on bacteriorhodopsin, cytochrome Bc1 and OprM (outer membrane efflux protein from Pseudomonas aeruginosa), is efficient to immobilized untagged membrane proteins that cannot be produced in a recombinant form. In vivo Avi-tagged and biotinylated membrane proteins can also be immobilized in the presence of detergents for phage display selections, as shown with the selection of MexB-specific αRep binders [8].
Specific αRep binders could be obtained for a panel of chosen proteins of unrelated structures, indicating that the $\alpha$ Rep library can give rise to specific binders for a wide range of targets of interest. As the αRep libraries contain short proteins, with few repeats, as well as long proteins with more repeats, it was not known what size of $\alpha$ Reps would be the preferential source of binders. The experimental results show that αRep binders of different sizes with, 2–7 repeats are commonly selected. Therefore, it appears that short as well as
The αRep library can be used to select by phage display αRep binding to a wide range of protein targets. Specific αRep proteins can be used as crystallization chaperones, to generate affinity purification media and for the detection/modulation of protein targets inside living cells.
long repeat proteins from the library are an efficient source of binders. Successful selections are clearly not related to the fraction of the sequence space actually sampled in the experimental library. Indeed, the sequence space is much wider for long protein than for shorter proteins and is experimentally incompletely sampled in the library. This clearly does not prevent successful selections of long proteins. The sparse sampling of sequence space corresponding to long proteins may explain, however why the number of repeats does not appear to be correlated with the binding affinity. For example, bA3-2 can bind its target αRep-A3 with a $K_{\mathrm{D}}$ of $16~\mathrm{{nM}}$ with only one internal repeat whereas bA3-17 has a lower affinity $(K_{\mathrm{D}}=141\ \mathrm{nM})$ ) with two internal repeats [6]. GFP binders with respectively 3, 4 and 6 repeats all bind their target with a $K_{\mathrm{D}}$ in the low nanomolar range [9].
The structures of several αRep–target complexes were solved by $\mathrm{x}$ -ray crystallography. Our results show that αReps crystallize efficiently in complex with model targets presenting different topologies: an all- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\alpha_{} $ repeat protein (A3; Figure 2A), a $\beta$ -sandwich (NCS-3.24; Figure 2B) and a $\beta$ - barrel target (the GFP: EGFP; Figure 2C). A3 is an αRep protein randomly picked from the library with a binding surface resulting from helices juxtaposition. NCS-3.24 is an engineered variant of a bacterial protein, the NCS secreted by Streptomyces neocarzinostaticus [10,11]. One specific binder of NCS-3.24 was selected and no interaction was observed with the NCS – WT (wild-type), although its overall structure is very similar and differs only by seven residues. Thus, αReps typically bind convex surfaces, although in some cases (NCS-3.24) concave surfaces such as binding sites can also be discriminated with a high specificity. The structures of two binders in complex with the EGFP target were solved by X-ray crystallography and revealed that the high affinity of these two binders actually results from two completely different binding modes [9].
## $\pmb{\alpha}$ Rep proteins applications Crystallization chaperones
αReps have been successfully used as crystallization chaperones for proteins that could not crystallize alone (Figure 2D and Figure 3). High-affinity interactions of a target protein with a specific partner can modulate in a very significant way its solubility and its stability. Furthermore, the new surface provided by the binder gives rise to new possibilities of intermolecular contacts in the crystal lattice and consequently can strongly influence the crystallization properties of the target protein [12]. αReps can also help for structural studies of proteins recalcitrant to crystallization. For example, no good-quality crystals could be obtained from highly purified recombinant full-length FNE. FNE binders were selected by phage display and a selected binder $(K_{\mathrm{D}}=0.18\mu\mathrm{M})$ was used for the crystallization trials. The structure of the complex FNE–αRep was solved [1.8 A˚ ( $1\mathrm{~\AA~}=0.1\mathrm{nm}$ ) resolution] revealing that mobile loop regions of FNE are stabilized by αRep binding thus generating an appropriate surface for intermolecular contacts and crystallization (Figure 2D) [7]. This strategy could also be promising for membrane proteins, which are difficult cases for structural studies.
## New cellular tools
We have recently showed that αRep proteins can be used in living cells (Figure 3) [9]. This is an important advantage compared with most antibodies or derivatives, which are often poorly expressed in cells. Using EGFP as a target, we have first demonstrated that the affinity and specificity of three different αRep binders were sufficient to purify GFP-fusion proteins from a cell extract in pulldown experiments. Furthermore, these three variants can be stably expressed in mammalian cells. These proteins are folded and soluble in the cell cytoplasm and can also diffuse into the nucleus. Finally, we have established that GFP specific αReps co-localize with EGFP targeted to different cell compartments (Golgi, nucleus, mitochondria). In these experiments, αReps can accurately report the localization of a target protein in living cells without forming aggregates or causing toxicity, as often observed when using antibodies.
αRep proteins appears therefore to be very well adapted to explore intracellular processes in living cells, by interacting tightly and specifically with endogenous proteins.
